dsa - Data structures and algorithms (tracks/dsa)
-------------------------------------------------

Upstream   None. The tools are written from the documented behaviour of
           the ones they copy and checked against the machine's own: GNU
           coreutils on Linux (https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/,
           GPL-3.0-or-later) and the FreeBSD utilities macOS ships
           (https://github.com/freebsd/freebsd-src, BSD licences). No
           code from either is in the track.
           diff and patch are also held to GNU diffutils
           (https://www.gnu.org/software/diffutils/) and GNU patch
           (https://savannah.gnu.org/projects/patch/), both
           GPL-3.0-or-later, and to the diff and patch macOS ships; no
           code from any of them is in the track. lib/myers.c is written
           from the paper: Eugene W. Myers, "An O(ND) Difference Algorithm
           and Its Variations", Algorithmica 1 (1986), 251-266.
           grep is held to GNU grep (https://www.gnu.org/software/grep/,
           GPL-3.0-or-later) and to the BSD grep macOS ships
           (BSD-2-Clause); no code from either is in the track. It is
           written from POSIX's definition of extended regular
           expressions and the published algorithms: Horspool's search
           (1980), Thompson's construction (1968), and a DFA built as it
           runs, as Russ Cox describes RE2's.
           gzip and gunzip are written from the two specifications, RFC
           1951 (DEFLATE) and RFC 1952 (gzip), both by L. Peter Deutsch,
           May 1996 (https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1951 and
           /rfc1952), and held to GNU gzip
           (https://www.gnu.org/software/gzip/, GPL-3.0-or-later) and the
           gzip macOS ships. No text of either RFC and no code of zlib,
           GNU gzip or any other program is in the track; what comes from
           the RFCs is what the formats are made of (the code tables and
           header fields). tracks/dsa/tools/gzip/NOTICE.txt has the
           details.
           make is held to GNU make (https://www.gnu.org/software/make/,
           GPL-3.0-or-later; 3.81 on macOS, 4.4.1 on Linux) on the
           features this track's own Makefile uses; no code from it is in
           the track.
           git (module 10) is written to git's repository formats as git
           documents them (loose objects, trees, commits, the version 2
           index: Pro Git chapter 10 and gitformat-index(5)), with SHA-1
           from FIPS PUB 180-4 and zlib framing from RFC 1950, and held
           to the real git (https://github.com/git/git, GPL-2.0), which
           reads and checks the repositories it writes. No code of git,
           zlib or any other program is in the track.
           tracks/dsa/tools/git/NOTICE.txt has the details.
Upstream   For module 03 only (tools/edit): kilo, by Salvatore Sanfilippo
           (antirez), https://github.com/antirez/kilo (master, commit
           323d93b29bd89a2cb446de90c4ed4fea1764176e), by way of this
           course's kilo track (tracks/kilo).
Licence    BSD-2-Clause. Copyright (c) 2016, Salvatore Sanfilippo
           <antirez at gmail dot com>. The full text is in
           tracks/dsa/tools/edit/LICENSE, which comes with the project's
           files from lesson 03.01 on; edit.c keeps the notice in its
           header.
Taken      tools/edit/edit.c: the kilo track's kilo.c as its core leaves it,
           without the track's extensions (raw mode,
           reading keys, drawing the screen, moving the cursor, search,
           saving), with its rows replaced by the text interface of
           lib/text.h, undo and redo keys, and a replay mode with no
           terminal. The changes are listed in tools/edit/NOTES.md.
Ours       All of the code but tools/edit/edit.c (lib/, tools/, bench/, data/gen.py, the
           Makefile and the tool.mk files), the lessons and the internal
           checks (tracks/dsa/track.py and tests/).

