research - AI research, from a first experiment to your own result
(tracks/research)
------------------------------------------------------------------

Upstream   None. The engine (engine/) is written from scratch in numpy and
           takes no code from PyTorch (https://github.com/pytorch/pytorch,
           BSD-3-Clause). It uses PyTorch's names and argument order
           (Tensor, nn.Module, nn.Linear, F.cross_entropy, optim.AdamW, ...)
           so that one script runs on either by changing one import line,
           and it is checked against PyTorch: the internal tests compare
           its values and gradients with PyTorch's, and run every
           experiment on PyTorch as well. Up to lesson 06.01 PyTorch is
           used by the internal tests only and the project needs only
           numpy; from 06.02 on the project's scripts import PyTorch
           itself, which the learner installs (none of it is in this
           repository).
Format     safetensors (https://github.com/huggingface/safetensors,
           Apache-2.0): lab/safetensors.py writes and reads checkpoints in
           that file format (a header length, a JSON header, the raw
           numbers), with the names of the library's numpy functions
           (save_file, load_file). The format only: no code of the library
           is in the track. The internal tests use the library to open
           what the track writes, and the other way round.
Ours       All of the code (engine/, lab/, data/, models/, experiments/),
           the recorded long run (experiments/recorded/), the lessons, and
           the internal checks (tracks/research/track.py and tests/). Lessons that run a paper's claim again name the paper
           and link to it; no text or figure of a paper is copied, and
           every curve is drawn from the track's own runs.

Data       MNIST, the handwritten digits of Yann LeCun, Corinna Cortes and
           Christopher J. C. Burges: the four original .gz files,
           11,594,722 bytes. Not in this repository, and never downloaded
           by a build, a test or a lesson: the learner fetches them once,
           with python3 -m data.fetch mnist (tracks/research/data/fetch.py),
           from the mirror torchvision uses
           (https://ossci-datasets.s3.amazonaws.com/mnist/), into a cache
           outside the project, and a file is kept only if its size and
           SHA-256 match the ones pinned in fetch.py.
Licence    As read at the source on 2026-11-14: MNIST's own page states no
           licence and no longer serves the files; Keras gives CC BY-SA
           3.0, Hugging Face MIT. Nothing of it is redistributed here.
Data       CIFAR-10, by Alex Krizhevsky, Vinod Nair and Geoffrey Hinton:
           the binary version, one archive of 170,052,171 bytes (its MD5
           matches the one the dataset's page publishes). Not in this
           repository, and never downloaded by a build, a test or a
           lesson: the learner fetches it once, with python3 -m data.fetch
           cifar10, from the dataset's own page
           (https://www.cs.toronto.edu/~kriz/cifar.html), into a cache
           outside the project; the archive and each of the seven files
           taken out of it are kept only if their sizes and SHA-256 match
           the ones pinned in fetch.py.
Licence    As read at the source on 2026-11-14 and 2026-11-24 (the page now
           redirects to cave.cs.toronto.edu): CIFAR-10's page states no
           licence and asks that the tech report be cited (Krizhevsky,
           "Learning Multiple Layers of Features from Tiny Images", 2009).
           Nothing of it is redistributed here.
Data       Tiny Shakespeare: a selection of Shakespeare's plays, 1,115,394
           bytes of plain ASCII, put together by Andrej Karpathy for
           char-rnn (https://github.com/karpathy/char-rnn, the file
           data/tinyshakespeare/input.txt, unchanged since the
           repository's first commit; fetched at commit
           6f9487a6fe5b420b7ca9afb0d7c078e37c1d1b4e). It ships with the
           track, byte for byte, as tracks/research/data/tinyshakespeare.txt
           (SHA-256 86c4e6aa9db7c042ec79f339dcb96d42b0075e16b8fc2e86bf0ca57e2dc565ed),
           and lessons from 07.01 on train and score on it.
Licence    As read at the source on 2026-11-24: the plays are in the public
           domain; the char-rnn repository's Readme gives its licence as
           MIT. The file is redistributed here unchanged.
Generated  The two groups of points (data/points.py) are made from a seed
           when a script runs; none is stored.


